Quoth Erik Hesselink <sel4yixa02DONT@SPAMsneakemail.com> on this day
of Tue, 13 Jan 2004 14:19:50 +0100:

[protection racket for gnomish healers]

>Have you tried it? I find it pretty easy (I think 50% of my attempted 
>pacifists make it to minetown). 

Hmm. 50% success rate of making it to minetown isn't really all that
great for an experienced player; I think that that in itself is a
concession of the difficulty of the strategy.

But my issue really isn't making it *to* minetown. It's what happens
*after* you get this allegedly great bonus. Because even with great
(for early game) AC, some hits are still going to get through. And if
your hp are so low that  you're encountering monsters that can kill
you in one turn, that's a real problem.

I'd really like to hear from some protection-racketeers about what
their strategies (and success rates) are *after* they've bought
protection. What do they do next? At what point do they decide to
start levelling up? Do they really end up getting much further in the
dungeon than they would have if they hadn't used that strategy? 

I just have a sneaking suspicion that this
getting-to-minetown-and-donating-to-the-priest is kind of a mini-quest
for some people; if they make it, they've "succeeded," even if their
character dies soon afterward. I only say this because of my own
experiences (of having my character die soon after donating for
protection) and because of the number of people offering advice with a
tag on the end of their post to the effect of, "Of course, I've never
ascended, but ...." That's not to say that mid-level players can't
offer helpful advice to newbies, but sometimes strategies that seem
helpful at one stage turn out to be not so good at getting you beyond
that stage.
